🚽 Test case: round-24 review finding #1's user-defined-call sibling --
🚽 the same global-return-slot reentrancy doesn't require a native call
🚽 at all: `three()` (declared `rizz`/int) also overwrites current_
🚽 return_value while `widen()` (declared `gigachad`/double) is still
🚽 mid-evaluation of its own `bussin three();`. Confirmed via a temporary
🚽 A/B test (git stash to the pre-fix commit) that this printed
🚽 "0.000000" instead of "3.000000", same failure mode as the native-call
🚽 exhibit, proving the bug is in the user-function return channel
🚽 itself, not anything native-ABI-specific.
rizz three() {
    bussin 3;
}

gigachad widen() {
    bussin three();
}

skibidi main {
    gigachad y = widen();
    yappin("%f\n", y);
    bussin 0;
}
